00:09:59.410 --> 00:10:00.900
they could just go without having to pay

00:10:00.900 --> 00:10:03.330
and that was way before E-Zpass.

00:10:03.330 --> 00:10:04.163
- [Gary] I love that.

00:10:04.163 --> 00:10:08.090
- Anyways, my whole family
was very patronizing

00:10:08.090 --> 00:10:10.710
of my early entrepreneurial ventures

00:10:11.863 --> 00:10:15.860
but in high school started
a little apparel company

00:10:15.860 --> 00:10:16.780
called Live Big.

00:10:16.780 --> 00:10:18.650
My friends initials were b-i-g

00:10:18.650 --> 00:10:20.570
so we made Live Big

00:10:20.570 --> 00:10:22.370
and then it became nothing

00:10:22.370 --> 00:10:23.746
but we owned livebig.com

00:10:23.746 --> 00:10:26.510
and then sophomore year of college,

00:10:26.510 --> 00:10:27.810
I get an email from--

00:10:27.810 --> 00:10:29.550
- When did you own livebig.com?

00:10:29.550 --> 00:10:31.990
- Back in 1996.

00:10:31.990 --> 00:10:33.290
- Very good, keep going

00:10:33.290 --> 00:10:35.757
when I registered winelibrary.com,

00:10:35.757 --> 00:10:37.470
very cool.
- Right around.

00:10:37.470 --> 00:10:38.810
- You know what's really
interesting about this?

00:10:38.810 --> 00:10:40.640
This is kind of super weird.

00:10:40.640 --> 00:10:42.843
You were in high school
you said in Newton?

00:10:43.730 --> 00:10:47.541
That probably means that
you and I both registered

00:10:47.541 --> 00:10:51.780
in 1996, livebig.com and winelibrary.com

00:10:51.780 --> 00:10:53.420
in Newton, Mass.

00:10:53.420 --> 00:10:54.253
- Wild.

00:10:54.253 --> 00:10:56.074
- I like that, that makes me happy.

00:10:56.074 --> 00:10:57.200
- [Scott] The building registry was like,

00:10:57.200 --> 00:10:58.033
what's all this activity?

00:10:58.033 --> 00:10:59.800
- I'm trying to think about which,

00:10:59.800 --> 00:11:01.310
was there network solutions?

00:11:01.310 --> 00:11:03.563
- Network solutions
was the way you did it.

00:11:03.563 --> 00:11:05.902
- That's how you did it too?
- [Scott] Yeah.

00:11:05.902 --> 00:11:06.900
- The recall.

00:11:06.900 --> 00:11:08.732
- 100%. Now had I gone
back there I would've--

00:11:08.732 --> 00:11:10.230
- Guys, pre-godaddy.

00:11:10.230 --> 00:11:11.780
- No it was a pain in the ass

00:11:11.780 --> 00:11:14.230
to get a domain at that time.
- Yeah did you have to

00:11:14.230 --> 00:11:15.120
fax something?

00:11:15.120 --> 00:11:16.820
- There was probably a fax
with a signature involved

00:11:16.820 --> 00:11:18.621
and it took awhile to process.

00:11:18.621 --> 00:11:21.150
- Yeah, you youngsters
have no fucking idea.

00:11:21.150 --> 00:11:23.460
This is our version of
we walked in the snow.

00:11:23.460 --> 00:11:25.950
We had to fax to get our websites, kids.

00:11:25.950 --> 00:11:26.783
Keep going.

00:11:27.694 --> 00:11:29.270
- Anyways, this became nothing.

00:11:29.270 --> 00:11:31.980
However, sophomore of
college, I get this email

00:11:31.980 --> 00:11:34.040
from an RV company.

00:11:34.040 --> 00:11:36.090
You know those big things
that you live in, right?

00:11:36.090 --> 00:11:37.000
And then they're like--

00:11:37.000 --> 00:11:39.043
- What do you think I'm
like some trillionaire

00:11:39.043 --> 00:11:40.041
in a fucking ivory tower?

00:11:40.041 --> 00:11:41.270
I know what a fucking RV is.
- Just making the connections.

00:11:41.270 --> 00:11:42.930
Big live, live in something big,

00:11:42.930 --> 00:11:45.170
they were like we want to buy Live Big

00:11:45.170 --> 00:11:47.913
and I was like well, it's
not just a domain name,

00:11:47.913 --> 00:11:48.746
it's a company.

00:11:48.746 --> 00:11:53.746
And so I think I negotiated
a $7,000 acquisition

00:11:53.880 --> 00:11:55.593
of my first company.

00:11:55.593 --> 00:11:57.216
I was pretty psyched

00:11:57.216 --> 00:11:59.080
'cause I was like
sophomore year of college,

00:11:59.080 --> 00:12:00.710
my company got acquired

00:12:00.710 --> 00:12:03.510
and my friend Ben was at Princeton

00:12:03.510 --> 00:12:05.940
and it was one of those
moments where we were like--

00:12:05.940 --> 00:12:08.800
- Were you literally walking around campus

00:12:08.800 --> 00:12:11.701
like yo baby, my company
just was acquired?

00:12:11.701 --> 00:12:13.075
- Kind of a big deal

00:12:13.075 --> 00:12:15.603
but it was a first foray,

00:12:16.693 --> 00:12:18.210
even if it was a very small micro journey,

00:12:18.210 --> 00:12:19.370
it was a journey nonetheless.

00:12:19.370 --> 00:12:20.490
- [Gary] It gave you a high?

00:12:20.490 --> 00:12:22.522
- Of what, what do you mean?

00:12:22.522 --> 00:12:23.600
- [Gary] Did you get a high on holy shit?

00:12:23.600 --> 00:12:25.660
- I definitely got some sort of high.

00:12:25.660 --> 00:12:27.840
It wasn't going anywhere

00:12:27.840 --> 00:12:32.321
but it was a taste of
coming up with something

00:12:32.321 --> 00:12:33.319
and doing something--
- Did that make you

00:12:33.319 --> 00:12:34.866
want to get into domain
naming for a second?

00:12:34.866 --> 00:12:37.060
That would've tricked me.

00:12:37.060 --> 00:12:38.640
- I always buy names.

00:12:38.640 --> 00:12:39.710
Whenever I had an idea,

00:12:39.710 --> 00:12:41.124
I'm sure you're the same.

00:12:41.124 --> 00:12:42.640
- Me too but I wasn't buying domains

00:12:42.640 --> 00:12:44.850
like a real estate portfolio.

00:12:44.850 --> 00:12:47.780
I'll never forget that
business 2.0 magazine

00:12:47.780 --> 00:12:50.793
that had the cover and it
said fish.com, $130 million.

00:12:51.641 --> 00:12:52.474
- We could have a whole episode

00:12:52.474 --> 00:12:54.240
about the crazy nature
of the domain industry

00:12:54.240 --> 00:12:56.240
and who the holders are
of all these domains

00:12:56.240 --> 00:12:58.050
and the way that they act.

00:12:58.050 --> 00:12:59.060
As you know, they're these brokers

00:12:59.060 --> 00:13:00.490
who go around with cases of cash

00:13:00.490 --> 00:13:02.830
trying to buy these things
for people and it's wild.

00:13:02.830 --> 00:13:05.560
But it's actually amazing
that it's still around but--

00:13:05.560 --> 00:13:06.393
- [Gary] Real estate.

00:13:06.393 --> 00:13:07.706
- It is real estate.

00:13:07.706 --> 00:13:11.870
Fast forward, graduated college,

00:13:11.870 --> 00:13:14.200
always had an interest
in design and business.

00:13:14.200 --> 00:13:15.950
In college, I studied
both design and business,

00:13:15.950 --> 00:13:17.280
had to choose one,

00:13:17.280 --> 00:13:19.838
ended up choosing Wall
Street for a few years,

00:13:19.838 --> 00:13:23.310
had one and a half years on
a traditional finance job.

00:13:23.310 --> 00:13:24.200
Hated it.

00:13:24.200 --> 00:13:25.730
And went to my manager and was like,

00:13:25.730 --> 00:13:27.179
I'm gonna leave

00:13:27.179 --> 00:13:28.210
and she was like,

00:13:28.210 --> 00:13:30.350
well if there was one thing
you could do in this firm,

00:13:30.350 --> 00:13:32.190
what would it be? And I was
like, I'm really interesting

00:13:32.190 --> 00:13:34.400
in understanding how
leaders make decisions

00:13:34.400 --> 00:13:35.440
and how a firm is managed

00:13:35.440 --> 00:13:36.610
and how problems are solved.

00:13:36.610 --> 00:13:39.200
And so I got this crazy
job at Golden Sachs

00:13:39.200 --> 00:13:40.540
in the executive office focusing

00:13:40.540 --> 00:13:42.840
on almost like internal
management consulting,

00:13:42.840 --> 00:13:44.630
when co-heads don't get along,

00:13:44.630 --> 00:13:47.100
when clients are going through struggles

00:13:47.100 --> 00:13:48.470
but they don't want to tell anyone else

00:13:48.470 --> 00:13:50.900
but they'll confide in the
firm and have their help.

00:13:50.900 --> 00:13:52.690
And that experience actually

00:13:52.690 --> 00:13:54.020
was a great education for me.

00:13:54.020 --> 00:13:54.853
- Interesting.

00:13:55.699 --> 00:13:57.530
How long did you do that?
- So that was three years.

00:13:57.530 --> 00:13:58.363
It was a great experience

00:13:58.363 --> 00:14:00.540
and I still actually pull from it

00:14:00.540 --> 00:14:01.373
which I think is rare.

00:14:01.373 --> 00:14:02.340
Usually out of college,

00:14:02.340 --> 00:14:03.290
you have bullshit jobs

00:14:03.290 --> 00:14:04.910
or you don't actually refer to anything.

00:14:04.910 --> 00:14:06.229
But fast forward,

00:14:06.229 --> 00:14:08.690
I left and started Behance.

00:14:08.690 --> 00:14:09.810
The idea of Behance,

00:14:09.810 --> 00:14:11.360
organize the creative world.

00:14:11.360 --> 00:14:13.421
I used to tell people
that and they were like,

00:14:13.421 --> 00:14:14.254
yeah, yeah, good luck with that.

00:14:14.254 --> 00:14:16.510
But Behance, long story short,

00:14:16.510 --> 00:14:18.550
became a network of over
15 million creatives

00:14:18.550 --> 00:14:19.490
showcasing their work,

00:14:19.490 --> 00:14:21.700
getting attribution for
the work that they did.

00:14:21.700 --> 00:14:23.550
Agencies typically take all the credit.

00:14:23.550 --> 00:14:25.020
Usually it's the creatives
at the end of the day

00:14:25.020 --> 00:14:26.410
that actually did a lot of the stuff

00:14:26.410 --> 00:14:27.992
and now they get opportunity.

00:14:27.992 --> 00:14:30.940
We were acquired by Adobe in 2012.

00:14:30.940 --> 00:14:32.270
- How long did you run that company?

00:14:32.270 --> 00:14:34.740
- So it was five years of bootstrapping

00:14:34.740 --> 00:14:37.910
which we could talk about
forever, that was rough.

00:14:37.910 --> 00:14:39.710
Two years as a venture backed company.

00:14:39.710 --> 00:14:41.376
- Who'd you take money
from? I don't recall.

00:14:41.376 --> 00:14:45.035
- So Union Square Ventures,
Jeff Bezos, Chris Dickson,

00:14:45.035 --> 00:14:47.400
and a bunch of other
angels back in the day.

00:14:47.400 --> 00:14:48.580
That was probably one of the other emails

00:14:48.580 --> 00:14:49.810
that got lost in your inbox.

00:14:49.810 --> 00:14:51.210
That was before--

00:14:51.210 --> 00:14:52.418
- I don't know about that.

00:14:52.418 --> 00:14:53.418
What year did you fund?

00:14:53.418 --> 00:14:54.536
- I'm just kidding, I'm just kidding.

00:14:54.536 --> 00:14:55.369
2007.

00:14:56.959 --> 00:14:58.133
- Yeah I don't think you emailed me yet.

00:14:58.133 --> 00:14:59.060
I wasn't cool enough.
- 2008, around there.

00:14:59.060 --> 00:15:00.510
Actually the funding was 2009.
- Unless you want

00:15:00.510 --> 00:15:02.524
to trade wine for equity.

00:15:02.524 --> 00:15:05.133
- That probably would've not helped us.

00:15:06.462 --> 00:15:09.410
Anyways, so yeah, that
was an incredible journey

00:15:09.410 --> 00:15:12.280
and one of the inspirations
for the Messy Middle

00:15:12.280 --> 00:15:13.880
was me actually thinking back

00:15:13.880 --> 00:15:16.400
to that five year period of bootstrapping,

00:15:16.400 --> 00:15:18.200
and two years as venture backed company.

00:15:18.200 --> 00:15:19.700
During 2008--

00:15:19.700 --> 00:15:22.070
- So the company started in 2002?

00:15:22.070 --> 00:15:22.903
- No sorry.

00:15:22.903 --> 00:15:26.680
So the company started in
2005, 2006 right around there

00:15:26.680 --> 00:15:29.930
and then we actually raised the real round

00:15:29.930 --> 00:15:33.040
in late 2010, 2011.

00:15:33.040 --> 00:15:34.270
- You got a little angel money in '07?

00:15:34.270 --> 00:15:38.773
- Yeah, from friends and
family but barely any.

00:15:40.170 --> 00:15:41.570
- And you sold to Adobe when?

00:15:41.570 --> 00:15:44.550
- In 2012, end of 2012.

00:15:44.550 --> 00:15:46.300
So it was quite a journey

00:15:46.300 --> 00:15:47.900
and I actually didn't,

00:15:47.900 --> 00:15:50.620
when I was sitting down
to think about this book

00:15:50.620 --> 00:15:53.060
which is really the aggregation
of five to seven years

00:15:53.060 --> 00:15:55.470
of little insights
around people's middles,

00:15:55.470 --> 00:15:58.170
friends of mine,
entrepreneurs, artists, writers

00:15:58.170 --> 00:16:00.630
who endured and optimized

00:16:00.630 --> 00:16:03.170
through a very volatile middle journey,

00:16:03.170 --> 00:16:05.020
I was like, well I
gotta start with my own.

00:16:05.020 --> 00:16:06.750
And I realized I didn't remember it.

00:16:06.750 --> 00:16:07.590
And actually what I did

00:16:07.590 --> 00:16:08.910
is I went back in my phone

00:16:08.910 --> 00:16:10.250
through all of the screenshots

00:16:10.250 --> 00:16:13.600
from the years 2006 to 2012

00:16:13.600 --> 00:16:15.730
just as like that's
probably the source of truth

00:16:15.730 --> 00:16:17.230
for where I was, what I was thinking

00:16:17.230 --> 00:16:18.210
and what I was doing.

00:16:18.210 --> 00:16:20.320
And I saw all the screenshots

00:16:20.320 --> 00:16:22.780
of people giving us a hard time online

00:16:22.780 --> 00:16:24.360
or people liking what we were doing online

00:16:24.360 --> 00:16:27.493
or errors or issues and team moments

00:16:27.493 --> 00:16:29.010
and tough moments and celebrations

00:16:29.010 --> 00:16:29.843
and all that stuff

00:16:29.843 --> 00:16:31.360
and actually that was the first moment

00:16:31.360 --> 00:16:33.520
where I got the granularity
of some of the stuff

00:16:33.520 --> 00:16:36.042
frankly that I had wanted to forget.

00:16:36.042 --> 00:16:37.680
- 100,000%.

00:16:37.680 --> 00:16:39.980
No, I think that's a
really interesting thesis.

00:16:39.980 --> 00:16:42.040
I think that in general,

00:16:42.040 --> 00:16:43.465
if you even think about a movie,

00:16:43.465 --> 00:16:46.090
people can think about
the beginnings of movies

00:16:46.090 --> 00:16:47.270
and how it ended.

00:16:47.270 --> 00:16:50.740
The middle is a very
fascinating part of everything

00:16:50.740 --> 00:16:53.050
when you look at it in its entirety.

00:16:53.050 --> 00:16:54.940
A lot of times you don't
know you're in the middle.

00:16:54.940 --> 00:16:55.773
It's funny, you're talking,

00:16:55.773 --> 00:16:58.035
I'm like, ugh, I'm in the middle

00:16:58.035 --> 00:17:01.127
of the whole Vayner X thing
right now, this is the muckery.

00:17:01.127 --> 00:17:03.380
Me and AJ part,

00:17:03.380 --> 00:17:05.630
and you knew AJ when he
was coming out the gate,

00:17:05.630 --> 00:17:07.580
that was fun and dadada

00:17:07.580 --> 00:17:10.090
and boy, do I think it's
going to be a monster

00:17:10.090 --> 00:17:11.620
and so there'll be that part

00:17:11.620 --> 00:17:14.730
but I'm squarely in the
fucking middle right now.

00:17:14.730 --> 00:17:15.730
- I think that,

00:17:15.730 --> 00:17:17.330
well hopefully we're always
in the middle to some extent

00:17:17.330 --> 00:17:20.080
'cause otherwise what
the heck are we doing.

00:17:20.080 --> 00:17:21.840
But I think it's also fascinating

00:17:21.840 --> 00:17:23.490
that yes, there's all this volatility,

00:17:23.490 --> 00:17:25.850
there's surfing endless ambiguity,

00:17:25.850 --> 00:17:26.880
working in anonymity,

00:17:26.880 --> 00:17:28.420
no one cares what you're doing,

00:17:28.420 --> 00:17:29.780
which actually is sometimes a good thing

00:17:29.780 --> 00:17:31.491
as well as a bad thing.

00:17:31.491 --> 00:17:32.324
- I would actually argue,

00:17:32.324 --> 00:17:33.460
this is a really interesting thought,

00:17:33.460 --> 00:17:35.910
I know that since we've been
in so many jams together,

00:17:35.910 --> 00:17:37.775
I know we're gonna go on a tangent here,

00:17:37.775 --> 00:17:39.960
I actually think that that's exactly right

00:17:39.960 --> 00:17:41.250
and as a strength

00:17:41.250 --> 00:17:44.370
and I actually think that
a lot of kids right now,

00:17:44.370 --> 00:17:45.600
and not just kids,

00:17:45.600 --> 00:17:48.500
are actually doing too
much content creation

00:17:48.500 --> 00:17:50.013
and self promotion

00:17:50.013 --> 00:17:52.620
which is a vulnerability
when you're in that middle

00:17:52.620 --> 00:17:55.670
meaning I posted the other day,

00:17:55.670 --> 00:17:58.010
being underestimated is the great gift

00:17:58.010 --> 00:18:00.130
and everybody spends all
their time trying to prove,

00:18:00.130 --> 00:18:02.310
especially and look at the youngsters,

00:18:02.310 --> 00:18:03.580
you're 22, you're 23,

00:18:03.580 --> 00:18:04.750
now everything is like trying

00:18:04.750 --> 00:18:06.770
to prove to your parents
and your contemporaries

00:18:06.770 --> 00:18:08.070
that you're good

00:18:08.070 --> 00:18:09.870
and you start doing a ton of shit.

00:18:09.870 --> 00:18:11.620
To me, that's such an interesting point,

00:18:11.620 --> 00:18:13.391
when you're in the Messy Middle,

00:18:13.391 --> 00:18:16.210
I think being head down,

00:18:16.210 --> 00:18:17.170
not being out there

00:18:17.170 --> 00:18:19.000
and not hearing other voices

00:18:19.000 --> 00:18:21.200
because that is your most vulnerable time,

00:18:21.200 --> 00:18:22.530
is a strength

00:18:22.530 --> 00:18:24.997
yet so many people are trying
to put their lives out there

00:18:24.997 --> 00:18:27.587
that they're getting a lot of signals

00:18:27.587 --> 00:18:29.500
and I think it's not,

00:18:29.500 --> 00:18:31.250
I think in the beginning and the end,

00:18:31.250 --> 00:18:32.083
there's a little bit more,

00:18:32.083 --> 00:18:33.430
it's a very interesting point.

00:18:33.430 --> 00:18:36.680
- I talk about the fact
that we're all governed

00:18:36.680 --> 00:18:38.490
by the short term reward system

00:18:38.490 --> 00:18:40.297
and from birth,

00:18:40.297 --> 00:18:43.150
we're constantly getting
our parents gratification

00:18:43.150 --> 00:18:44.390
and then the check on the test

00:18:44.390 --> 00:18:45.340
and the grade in the course

00:18:45.340 --> 00:18:48.550
and Fred Wilson, venture
capitalist in New York,

00:18:48.550 --> 00:18:50.377
at one of our early conferences said,

00:18:50.377 --> 00:18:51.737
"The two greatest addictions in life

00:18:51.737 --> 00:18:53.780
"are heroine and a weekly salary."

00:18:53.780 --> 00:18:56.120
And talking about unplugging
yourself from that

00:18:56.120 --> 00:18:58.764
and what the implications are of that.

00:18:58.764 --> 00:19:02.210
And I think that a lot of
enduring the middle journey

00:19:02.210 --> 00:19:04.020
is about hacking your reward system.

00:19:04.020 --> 00:19:04.950
But to your point,

00:19:04.950 --> 00:19:08.360
you don't want to have fake
wins that are motivating you,

00:19:08.360 --> 00:19:09.590
you don't want to put out content

00:19:09.590 --> 00:19:11.070
and feel like that means I'm winning.

00:19:11.070 --> 00:19:12.430
Actually you want to build something

00:19:12.430 --> 00:19:13.970
that's lasting and real,

00:19:13.970 --> 00:19:16.520
and actually I feel
like the best companies

00:19:16.520 --> 00:19:17.820
launch without the press,

00:19:17.820 --> 00:19:19.090
they actually get something out there

00:19:19.090 --> 00:19:20.470
and validate it

00:19:20.470 --> 00:19:22.610
and get something that's working so well

00:19:22.610 --> 00:19:24.020
and I feel like press is relative

00:19:24.020 --> 00:19:25.530
to when it's announced,
not when it happens.

00:19:25.530 --> 00:19:27.171
- Got it.

00:19:27.171 --> 00:19:29.220
Do you know I hate that
I'm ina point in my career

00:19:29.220 --> 00:19:33.051
that anything I start now
comes with expectation?

00:19:33.051 --> 00:19:35.380
Like one of the weird,

