- Containers and merchandising.

00:22:21.810 --> 00:22:23.030
- [Scott] Even if it's
a better freaking photo.

00:22:23.030 --> 00:22:24.190
- Containers and merchan,

00:22:24.190 --> 00:22:26.580
I don't even want to get into this, dude,

00:22:26.580 --> 00:22:30.040
I'm so into this fucking shit,
containers and merchandising,

00:22:30.040 --> 00:22:31.070
I'll get into that later.

00:22:31.070 --> 00:22:33.640
Messy Middle, who who's listening

00:22:33.640 --> 00:22:37.683
in a matrix of who's the ultimate person

00:22:37.683 --> 00:22:39.690
that 100% should buy this,

00:22:39.690 --> 00:22:40.523
who's the next,

00:22:40.523 --> 00:22:43.750
I like to think of books in
three tiers of a triangle,

00:22:43.750 --> 00:22:47.930
the top of the triangle is like
this will change your game,

00:22:47.930 --> 00:22:49.200
this is made for you.

00:22:49.200 --> 00:22:51.530
Then there's the big
middle of that of like

00:22:51.530 --> 00:22:52.730
this is probably probably for you

00:22:52.730 --> 00:22:55.700
and then the curious kind of nuanced one.

00:22:55.700 --> 00:22:57.430
So break that down for
me, one, two and three.

00:22:57.430 --> 00:22:59.970
Who is like listening
and watching right now,

00:22:59.970 --> 00:23:02.210
absolutely needs this?

00:23:02.210 --> 00:23:03.400
- I think that the more--

00:23:03.400 --> 00:23:05.118
- Oh shit, this is an ask show isn't it?

00:23:05.118 --> 00:23:06.420
- [Scott] We'll get to it.

00:23:06.420 --> 00:23:08.438
- Yeah, we're gonna get to it in a minute

00:23:08.438 --> 00:23:09.271
but answer that real quick.

00:23:09.271 --> 00:23:10.638
- It's good question.

00:23:10.638 --> 00:23:11.990
I think that the more volatile,

00:23:11.990 --> 00:23:13.840
the more this book is for you.

00:23:13.840 --> 00:23:16.118
I really do believe that there are,

00:23:16.118 --> 00:23:18.630
essentially everything
that everyone's doing

00:23:18.630 --> 00:23:20.660
is either a venture or replay.

00:23:20.660 --> 00:23:21.570
A venture in the sense

00:23:21.570 --> 00:23:23.280
that it's going into unknown territory,

00:23:23.280 --> 00:23:24.290
whether you're in a big company

00:23:24.290 --> 00:23:25.700
doing a product turnaround

00:23:25.700 --> 00:23:27.210
and doing something that's off the radar

00:23:27.210 --> 00:23:30.340
and you're fighting the immune
system of the big company

00:23:30.340 --> 00:23:31.740
or if you're just an entrepreneur

00:23:31.740 --> 00:23:33.850
starting something up and hustling,

00:23:33.850 --> 00:23:35.940
if you are venturing, this is for you.

00:23:35.940 --> 00:23:37.120
If you're doing the replay,

00:23:37.120 --> 00:23:38.330
playing someone else's playbook,

00:23:38.330 --> 00:23:40.106
probably not as relevant.

00:23:40.106 --> 00:23:41.430
I also would say

00:23:41.430 --> 00:23:44.210
that in the volatility side of things,

00:23:44.210 --> 00:23:47.650
the point of this book is
that we are not our best self

00:23:47.650 --> 00:23:48.890
at the lowest of the lows

00:23:48.890 --> 00:23:50.560
nor the highest of the highs.

00:23:50.560 --> 00:23:51.530
At the lowest of the lows,

00:23:51.530 --> 00:23:53.150
we're making decisions out of fear,

00:23:53.150 --> 00:23:54.313
we're losing hope,

00:23:55.164 --> 00:23:56.690
we're trying to manage
uncertainty and ambiguity,

00:23:56.690 --> 00:23:59.200
we're realizing we have to
short circuit our reward system.

00:23:59.200 --> 00:24:00.380
There's a ton in here

00:24:00.380 --> 00:24:01.930
about enduring the lows,

00:24:01.930 --> 00:24:03.990
mining them for the gems,

00:24:03.990 --> 00:24:05.670
and building the muscle memory

00:24:05.670 --> 00:24:07.740
to endure future lows

00:24:07.740 --> 00:24:10.745
and making every low incrementally higher.

00:24:10.745 --> 00:24:12.364
And then on the highs,

00:24:12.364 --> 00:24:13.900
the thing about the peaks

00:24:13.900 --> 00:24:16.520
is that then we start to falsely attribute

00:24:16.520 --> 00:24:19.670
the things that we did to us peaking.

00:24:19.670 --> 00:24:21.867
And so a lot of this also

00:24:21.867 --> 00:24:23.030
is about looking anything that works

00:24:23.030 --> 00:24:25.480
and then working like hell to optimize it

00:24:25.480 --> 00:24:27.810
and it's ironic that in the messy middle,

00:24:27.810 --> 00:24:30.180
when things work, we typically
don't do anything with it

00:24:30.180 --> 00:24:32.020
'cause we've got enough shit to deal with.

00:24:32.020 --> 00:24:34.542
But when actually something is working,

00:24:34.542 --> 00:24:35.375
whether it's how you're
managing your team,

00:24:35.375 --> 00:24:36.763
how you're hiring,

00:24:36.763 --> 00:24:37.596
how you're managing yourself,

00:24:37.596 --> 00:24:38.429
or something that's in your product

00:24:38.429 --> 00:24:40.610
that actually is getting
a little traction,

00:24:40.610 --> 00:24:43.640
on the contrary, we should
be iterating around that.

00:24:43.640 --> 00:24:45.060
And so this is a lot about

00:24:45.060 --> 00:24:47.260
optimizing the things
that work across team,

00:24:47.260 --> 00:24:48.480
product and self,

00:24:48.480 --> 00:24:49.987
how you're managing your own shit.

00:24:49.987 --> 00:24:52.030
Then there's a final section at the end

00:24:52.030 --> 00:24:53.260
called the final mile

00:24:53.260 --> 00:24:55.070
and this is question and insights

00:24:55.070 --> 00:24:57.550
around why so many people screw things up

00:24:57.550 --> 00:24:59.210
in the final mile of a project.

00:24:59.210 --> 00:25:00.960
There is the turnaround
we're about to launch,

00:25:00.960 --> 00:25:01.793
we don't want to mess this up,

00:25:01.793 --> 00:25:04.020
there's this self sabotage that happens

00:25:04.020 --> 00:25:06.400
after you're about to launch
something to the world.

00:25:06.400 --> 00:25:08.060
The questions of do I even deserve this

00:25:08.060 --> 00:25:09.450
and then subconsciously what that does

00:25:09.450 --> 00:25:10.283
to you and your team.

00:25:10.283 --> 00:25:12.540
I share some stories in there about that.

00:25:12.540 --> 00:25:14.020
- Give me an example, stick on this.

00:25:14.020 --> 00:25:15.955
- Yeah, on the self sabotage front,

00:25:15.955 --> 00:25:17.900
I talk about an example

00:25:17.900 --> 00:25:19.770
of somebody on my team

00:25:19.770 --> 00:25:21.960
who really in the final mile of Behance,

00:25:21.960 --> 00:25:22.950
when we were already in talks

00:25:22.950 --> 00:25:24.640
for acquisition and everything else

00:25:24.640 --> 00:25:28.520
just started to do a lot
of inappropriate stuff.

00:25:28.520 --> 00:25:31.586
And at first, it was like,
come on man, that's not good,

00:25:31.586 --> 00:25:33.830
you probably shouldn't say that to people,

00:25:33.830 --> 00:25:35.100
you probably shouldn't be doing that

00:25:35.100 --> 00:25:38.017
with these team members on
the weekend and whatever else.

00:25:38.017 --> 00:25:40.780
But it just kind of escalated.

00:25:40.780 --> 00:25:42.427
And I really started to
get very worried about it

00:25:42.427 --> 00:25:44.090
and people were coming to me about it

00:25:44.090 --> 00:25:45.250
and I was really getting curious.

00:25:45.250 --> 00:25:46.630
And I remember going home one night

00:25:46.630 --> 00:25:48.710
to my wife, Erica, who you know,

00:25:48.710 --> 00:25:49.590
who's a psychologist

00:25:49.590 --> 00:25:50.640
and kind of talking about it

00:25:50.640 --> 00:25:53.220
and she was just like, oh, this is easy,

00:25:53.220 --> 00:25:54.357
he doesn't feel like he deserves it.

00:25:54.357 --> 00:25:56.132
I'm like, what do you mean?

00:25:56.132 --> 00:25:57.410
We're all about to succeed,

00:25:57.410 --> 00:25:59.010
this is gonna be a great outcome for him.

00:25:59.010 --> 00:26:01.270
No, he's not comfortable with it.

00:26:01.270 --> 00:26:03.547
And at first, I was like, I don't know.

00:26:03.547 --> 00:26:05.986
So then the next day end of the day--

00:26:05.986 --> 00:26:07.230
- By the way, real quick,
just for everybody,

00:26:07.230 --> 00:26:09.030
this is my favorite part about life,

00:26:10.000 --> 00:26:12.640
she's a psychologist, you're
like doing this thing,

00:26:12.640 --> 00:26:14.100
you come home, she gives you the answer,

00:26:14.100 --> 00:26:14.940
you're like, nah.

00:26:14.940 --> 00:26:18.270
I have buddies, literally
this has happened to me,

00:26:18.270 --> 00:26:19.110
my apologies for jumping in

00:26:19.110 --> 00:26:20.330
'cause clearly I'm doing a better job

00:26:20.330 --> 00:26:21.210
than normally listening,

00:26:21.210 --> 00:26:23.170
speaks to why I love Scott,

00:26:23.170 --> 00:26:24.760
but the last week,

00:26:24.760 --> 00:26:27.510
I've had so many people
literally on the street grab me

00:26:27.510 --> 00:26:29.450
and be like, Gary, you say this,

00:26:29.450 --> 00:26:31.080
my homie Jerome says this.

00:26:31.080 --> 00:26:32.870
I'm like, well what's Jerome up to?

00:26:32.870 --> 00:26:33.703
Well, he sucks.

00:26:33.703 --> 00:26:35.720
I'm like, well don't you
think it's a good idea

00:26:35.720 --> 00:26:38.070
to think about who's giving the advice.

00:26:38.070 --> 00:26:39.700
Not all advice is equal.

00:26:39.700 --> 00:26:41.150
I just love that you come home,

00:26:41.150 --> 00:26:43.280
you respect your wife tremendously,
I know that for a fact,

00:26:43.280 --> 00:26:44.113
she's a psychologist,

00:26:44.113 --> 00:26:45.210
she tells you, oh, this is,

00:26:45.210 --> 00:26:48.500
listen to how he said
it, oh, this is easy,

00:26:48.500 --> 00:26:50.190
he's not okay with it.

00:26:50.190 --> 00:26:51.183
You're like, nah.

00:26:51.183 --> 00:26:53.970
- It doesn't make intuitive sense, right?

00:26:53.970 --> 00:26:55.490
- I understand.

00:26:55.490 --> 00:26:57.870
- But yeah, the point is

00:26:57.870 --> 00:27:00.343
at the final mile, it's a
different playbook altogether.

00:27:00.343 --> 00:27:01.176
- So what happened?

00:27:01.176 --> 00:27:02.336
Were you able to--

00:27:02.336 --> 00:27:03.981
- So I pulled him aside,
I pulled him aside,

00:27:03.981 --> 00:27:04.814
I sit down with him at the
corner of a conference room

00:27:04.814 --> 00:27:06.310
where no one could see us--

00:27:06.310 --> 00:27:08.053
