00:28:45.280 --> 00:28:46.740
I have a funny intuitive feeling,

00:28:46.740 --> 00:28:48.890
and I'm dying to see in four months

00:28:48.890 --> 00:28:50.250
when I stumble back to this

00:28:50.250 --> 00:28:52.080
or the comments or somebody tweets me

00:28:52.080 --> 00:28:53.080
and says, that was the right,

00:28:53.080 --> 00:28:55.513
I'm so intuitively curious will--

00:28:55.513 --> 00:28:56.830
- It's so funny you say this

00:28:56.830 --> 00:28:57.663
'cause two things.

00:28:57.663 --> 00:29:01.480
One is someone the other day
who read it at early galley

00:29:01.480 --> 00:29:03.000
said to me, you could substitute

00:29:03.000 --> 00:29:04.960
every business word in
there for relationship

00:29:04.960 --> 00:29:06.190
and it would be true.

00:29:06.190 --> 00:29:07.500
And then yesterday, I kid you not,

00:29:07.500 --> 00:29:08.640
I'm talking to somebody

00:29:08.640 --> 00:29:11.150
whose starting a wild company
in the fishing industry

00:29:11.150 --> 00:29:12.979
which I'm happy to tell
you about offline--

00:29:12.979 --> 00:29:14.759
- Interesting, I'm in.

00:29:14.759 --> 00:29:15.592
I've learned my lessons,

00:29:15.592 --> 00:29:16.953
where can I write a check?

00:29:16.953 --> 00:29:18.660
- What he said to me is like,

00:29:18.660 --> 00:29:20.110
I have to tell you the Messy Middle

00:29:20.110 --> 00:29:22.990
perfectly applies to fishing expeditions.

00:29:22.990 --> 00:29:24.100
I'm like, what are you talking about?

00:29:24.100 --> 00:29:25.610
He's like, my father's
a commercial fisherman

00:29:25.610 --> 00:29:28.910
and he always says,
there's great volatility

00:29:28.910 --> 00:29:31.080
from the start to the end
of a fishing expedition

00:29:31.080 --> 00:29:33.100
the last two weeks, deep sea fishing.

00:29:33.100 --> 00:29:34.680
He's like, the Messy
Middle perfectly applies.

00:29:34.680 --> 00:29:35.513
I'm like, really?

00:29:35.513 --> 00:29:37.421
I did not anticipate that.

00:29:37.421 --> 00:29:38.550
- Yeah, because of the Jets
tremendous win on Monday.

00:29:38.550 --> 00:29:40.179
When you first said that I was like,

00:29:40.179 --> 00:29:41.012
okay, this is the second or third quarter,

00:29:41.012 --> 00:29:42.623
this is perfect for football.

00:29:42.623 --> 00:29:43.923
- Listen, think that

00:29:43.923 --> 00:29:44.921
with parenting--
- Seth, let's get

00:29:44.921 --> 00:29:45.920
into a question.

00:29:45.920 --> 00:29:47.302
I feel like I've totally
derailed this show.

00:29:47.302 --> 00:29:48.300
What do you got?

00:29:48.300 --> 00:29:50.120
- [Seth] First question is
from First in Line group

00:29:50.120 --> 00:29:50.953
from Michael.

00:29:50.953 --> 00:29:52.633
- Micheal.
- [Seth] What are some tips

00:29:52.633 --> 00:29:54.530
for keeping your creative
team organized and productive

00:29:54.530 --> 00:29:55.970
without being a micromanager?

00:29:55.970 --> 00:29:57.790
I work to provide video
content for clients

00:29:57.790 --> 00:30:00.537
and between editors, social
media managers and clients,

00:30:00.537 --> 00:30:01.370
it can be a challenge.

00:30:01.370 --> 00:30:04.740
- Yeah, well listen, I
think that creative teams

00:30:04.740 --> 00:30:06.250
don't necessarily like to subscribe

00:30:06.250 --> 00:30:07.700
all to the same system

00:30:07.700 --> 00:30:09.230
and I think that's one of the reasons

00:30:09.230 --> 00:30:10.360
why so many of these companies

00:30:10.360 --> 00:30:12.750
that have built productivity
and project management tools,

00:30:12.750 --> 00:30:14.530
it's never gonna be a
winner take all market

00:30:14.530 --> 00:30:17.170
because most people talk
about switching costs

00:30:17.170 --> 00:30:19.287
like investors like Gary
and I will look and say,

00:30:19.287 --> 00:30:21.600
"Oh, is there a high switching
cost to this product,"

00:30:21.600 --> 00:30:23.070
and we'll retain its people.

00:30:23.070 --> 00:30:24.500
The funny thing about the creative world

00:30:24.500 --> 00:30:26.380
is there's actually switching benefits.

00:30:26.380 --> 00:30:28.850
When a creative team kind
of stops using a tool,

00:30:28.850 --> 00:30:30.363
it's almost like spring cleaning.

00:30:30.363 --> 00:30:33.780
Everyone will start to engage
and love the new product

00:30:33.780 --> 00:30:36.458
which is why you go
from Trello to Clubhouse

00:30:36.458 --> 00:30:39.290
to all these different kind
of methods and systems.

00:30:39.290 --> 00:30:41.140
So actually, I think
that for a creative team,

00:30:41.140 --> 00:30:42.340
you have to start paying attention

00:30:42.340 --> 00:30:46.103
to the elegance of the system.
You have to think about it,

00:30:46.103 --> 00:30:48.730
creatives are, we want
different colored folders,

00:30:48.730 --> 00:30:51.310
we want to have something that
we're visually attracted to.

00:30:51.310 --> 00:30:53.400
Attraction breeds loyalty over time.

00:30:53.400 --> 00:30:55.740
And so if you're a
program or project manager

00:30:55.740 --> 00:30:56.840
of a creative team

00:30:56.840 --> 00:30:59.150
and you're trying to manage
them with a spreadsheet,

00:30:59.150 --> 00:30:59.983
that will not work.

00:30:59.983 --> 00:31:01.800
You gotta be a little
more creative with it.

00:31:01.800 --> 00:31:03.160
- The only thing I'll jump in here on

00:31:03.160 --> 00:31:05.590
is I believe the
advancement of technology,

00:31:05.590 --> 00:31:08.087
a lot of people talk about
in the advertising world,

00:31:08.087 --> 00:31:09.980
personalization.

00:31:09.980 --> 00:31:11.130
I see Jets ads,

00:31:11.130 --> 00:31:12.960
you see ads for beard oil,

00:31:12.960 --> 00:31:15.760
it gets that good that we get there.

00:31:15.760 --> 00:31:18.350
I think that's great
and exciting and fine.

00:31:18.350 --> 00:31:21.590
I'm so much more into that in management.

00:31:21.590 --> 00:31:26.590
I genuinely think what the
miss is in a million ways

00:31:27.520 --> 00:31:32.420
is we make such general
statements about things.

00:31:32.420 --> 00:31:35.360
The only way to manage a team perfectly

00:31:35.360 --> 00:31:38.300
is to manage them on an individual level,

00:31:38.300 --> 00:31:39.870
on an every day basis,

00:31:39.870 --> 00:31:40.710
and having a pulse

00:31:40.710 --> 00:31:43.270
of their ambitions,
wants, desires and needs

00:31:43.270 --> 00:31:44.670
in an every second basis.

00:31:44.670 --> 00:31:45.503
I'm not kidding,

00:31:45.503 --> 00:31:46.890
that is how you actually win it.

00:31:46.890 --> 00:31:48.700
Now, how you actually do that--

00:31:48.700 --> 00:31:50.490
- [Scott] Yeah, how do
you scale it, I agree.

00:31:50.490 --> 00:31:53.603
- But I think you scale
it with fucking people.

00:31:54.880 --> 00:31:57.030
Just speak more often,

00:31:57.030 --> 00:31:57.980
watch more often,

00:31:57.980 --> 00:31:59.260
the way I scale it,

00:31:59.260 --> 00:32:01.690
I follow my people on social.

00:32:01.690 --> 00:32:02.870
I'll give you an example.

00:32:02.870 --> 00:32:05.070
I look at review cycles here

00:32:05.070 --> 00:32:06.990
and I see people reviewing each other.

00:32:06.990 --> 00:32:09.500
I know that they're hiking
with each other on the weekend

00:32:09.500 --> 00:32:12.120
which means that review is
better than it's supposed to be

00:32:12.120 --> 00:32:13.400
because those fuckers are homies.

00:32:13.400 --> 00:32:15.219
- [Scott] Mhmm, it's context.

00:32:15.219 --> 00:32:17.802
- Of course, context is the
fucking gateway to all of this.

00:32:17.802 --> 00:32:21.440
And so my two cents on this is like, look,

00:32:21.440 --> 00:32:22.273
when I hear people say,

00:32:22.273 --> 00:32:23.770
"Gary, how do I manage my team of 30?"

00:32:23.770 --> 00:32:25.837
I'm like, "Go to breakfast,
lunch and dinner,

00:32:25.837 --> 00:32:28.350
"make it a family, know shit."

00:32:28.350 --> 00:32:30.130
Because things change.

00:32:30.130 --> 00:32:32.220
Seth today may want more money

00:32:32.220 --> 00:32:35.496
but when he starts a
family may want more time.

00:32:35.496 --> 00:32:38.920
Somebody might want more recognition today

00:32:38.920 --> 00:32:39.880
'cause they're insecure

00:32:39.880 --> 00:32:41.890
or they want to leverage
it for another job.

00:32:41.890 --> 00:32:44.580
Later, they want more
privacy and don't want it.

00:32:44.580 --> 00:32:46.540
- I agree, it's on a
person by person basis

00:32:46.540 --> 00:32:47.955
and it's a problem.

00:32:47.955 --> 00:32:49.370
Managers feel like they
have to start scaling

00:32:49.370 --> 00:32:51.530
every way that they manage as they grow

00:32:51.530 --> 00:32:52.800
and in fact, to your point,

00:32:52.800 --> 00:32:53.633
it's really about--

00:32:53.633 --> 00:32:56.933
- And then tools become band-aids, right?

00:32:57.770 --> 00:32:58.603
Absolutely.

00:32:58.603 --> 00:32:59.847
- [Scott] Agreed.

00:32:59.847 --> 00:33:02.030
- [Seth] Next one also from
First in Line from Waleed.

00:33:02.030 --> 00:33:02.863
- Waleed.

00:33:03.707 --> 00:33:04.540
- [Seth] "There seems to be a large gap

00:33:04.540 --> 00:33:06.012
"between various industries

00:33:06.012 --> 00:33:07.007
"in terms of digital marketing strategies.

00:33:07.007 --> 00:33:08.697
"Some industries still
view digital marketing

00:33:08.697 --> 00:33:09.897
"in a very primitive way

00:33:09.897 --> 00:33:11.617
"while others are taking full advantage

00:33:11.617 --> 00:33:12.797
"and implementing solutions

00:33:12.797 --> 00:33:14.547
"from recruitment to brand affinity

00:33:15.608 --> 00:33:16.900
"in business decision making."

00:33:16.900 --> 00:33:18.447
- Business decision making, got it.

00:33:18.447 --> 00:33:19.617
- [Seth] "How can we bridge these gaps

00:33:19.617 --> 00:33:21.930
"and further the knowledge
of these industries?"

00:33:21.930 --> 00:33:22.910
- I mean, you're the expert.

00:33:22.910 --> 00:33:24.090
I have thoughts but go ahead.

00:33:24.090 --> 00:33:25.520
- [Gary] Why don't you
go with your thoughts?

00:33:25.520 --> 00:33:27.270
- No, I think that,

00:33:27.270 --> 00:33:28.510
well my day job now,

00:33:28.510 --> 00:33:30.103
which we didn't really talk about--

00:33:30.103 --> 00:33:30.940
- [Gary] Yes that's right,
what are you up to now?

00:33:30.940 --> 00:33:34.451
- I came back and jumped in as a new--

00:33:34.451 --> 00:33:35.530
- [Gary] You did BC for a little bit.

00:33:35.530 --> 00:33:36.529
- What's that?

00:33:36.529 --> 00:33:37.362
- [Gary] You did BC for a little bit.

00:33:37.362 --> 00:33:39.870
- Yeah, so Behance, acquired
by Adobe, three years,

00:33:39.870 --> 00:33:42.467
had the best time building
products for creative people.

00:33:42.467 --> 00:33:43.727
Everyone kept saying,

00:33:43.727 --> 00:33:45.610
because of the seed investing,

00:33:45.610 --> 00:33:47.805
you've got to be an investor,
you've got to be an investor

00:33:47.805 --> 00:33:48.638
and I actually made a mistake

00:33:48.638 --> 00:33:51.204
which was I started to
believe everyone around me

00:33:51.204 --> 00:33:52.720
in telling me what I should--

00:33:52.720 --> 00:33:55.664
- Hey listen, I was a
very loud voice in this.

00:33:55.664 --> 00:33:57.510
- To Gary's credit actually,

00:33:57.510 --> 00:34:01.728
Gary knew better than me at this point.

00:34:01.728 --> 00:34:04.110
Part of me felt like I had to try it.

00:34:04.110 --> 00:34:06.280
If everyone's telling me I
should be a venture investor,

00:34:06.280 --> 00:34:07.730
I should just do it full time.

00:34:07.730 --> 00:34:08.920
Jumped in full time.

00:34:08.920 --> 00:34:11.610
Three weeks in, I'm like, I'm
not building products anymore,

00:34:11.610 --> 00:34:12.990
I'm not working with teams anymore,

00:34:12.990 --> 00:34:14.200
I'm working in isolation,

00:34:14.200 --> 00:34:15.400
I'm trying to win the deal,

00:34:15.400 --> 00:34:16.730
I'm playing this transactional game,

00:34:16.730 --> 00:34:19.053
I don't know if this is me. I
don't know if I want to spend

00:34:19.053 --> 00:34:20.630
the next 15 years of my life doing it

00:34:20.630 --> 00:34:22.460
yet I was at one of the best

00:34:22.460 --> 00:34:23.810
venture capitalist firms in the world

00:34:23.810 --> 00:34:25.740
learning, enjoying every single day

00:34:25.740 --> 00:34:28.910
of absorbing from the folks
around the table at Benchmark

00:34:28.910 --> 00:34:30.270
and then I had this kind of question

00:34:30.270 --> 00:34:33.530
about but can I do this for 15
years and be the best at this

00:34:33.530 --> 00:34:34.720
or should I go back to building--

00:34:34.720 --> 00:34:36.810
- Look, I'm just gonna jump
in and just get to the chase.

00:34:36.810 --> 00:34:38.630
My level of respect for Scott

00:34:38.630 --> 00:34:40.850
was already pretty obnoxiously high

00:34:40.850 --> 00:34:41.683
in the scheme of things

00:34:41.683 --> 00:34:43.188
'cause of the serendipity

00:34:43.188 --> 00:34:44.021
of actually spending some time,

00:34:44.021 --> 00:34:45.688
'cause I'm sure there's
plenty of other people

00:34:45.688 --> 00:34:46.684
I'd love to respect,

00:34:46.684 --> 00:34:48.660
I just don't spend time with
people in the scheme of things,

00:34:48.660 --> 00:34:50.450
and then who he ended up being,

00:34:50.450 --> 00:34:53.600
the fact, I couldn't
over communicate enough

00:34:53.600 --> 00:34:55.400
to the people watching and listening,

00:34:55.400 --> 00:34:59.080
the level of the plum, plum, plum job

00:34:59.080 --> 00:35:02.462
in the game of venture
capital that Scott got.

00:35:02.462 --> 00:35:05.827
So I'm so weird that I
don't know things very well,

00:35:05.827 --> 00:35:07.646
like you guys kind of know me,

00:35:07.646 --> 00:35:11.790
he gets it, fine, I hear about it

00:35:11.790 --> 00:35:14.360
but other people that I massively respect

00:35:14.360 --> 00:35:16.240
and think are super smart and great,

00:35:16.240 --> 00:35:18.099
were like, wow.

00:35:18.099 --> 00:35:20.870
And I was like, oh fuck, it
must be really, really big.

00:35:20.870 --> 00:35:22.792
To have the ability to land that

00:35:22.792 --> 00:35:24.780
and then to have the self awareness

00:35:24.780 --> 00:35:27.490
and be willing to deal
with the scarlet letter

00:35:27.490 --> 00:35:30.440
that the micro community
would look and put on you

00:35:30.440 --> 00:35:31.623
when you left it,

00:35:33.583 --> 00:35:37.660
like dude, for you, I can't,

00:35:37.660 --> 00:35:39.210
this is a very big deal, Scott,

00:35:41.120 --> 00:35:43.240
for you to get it,

00:35:43.240 --> 00:35:45.120
and then leave it fairly early,

00:35:45.120 --> 00:35:47.640
very quickly in the scheme of things,

00:35:47.640 --> 00:35:49.010
which in a lot of ways

00:35:49.010 --> 00:35:51.090
could've been viewed as disrespectful,

00:35:51.090 --> 00:35:52.640
and a lot of different ways,

00:35:52.640 --> 00:35:55.713
especially in the micro
community of venture capital,

00:35:55.713 --> 00:35:58.230
to have the internal strength

00:35:58.230 --> 00:36:01.653
to know oh darn it, this is not who I am,

00:36:02.630 --> 00:36:04.990
and be able to make that kind of move,

00:36:04.990 --> 00:36:06.390
it is a much bigger deal

00:36:06.390 --> 00:36:08.814
than I think people give you credit for.

00:36:08.814 --> 00:36:11.637
- Listen, it was a, you could
have a whole episode on this,

00:36:11.637 --> 00:36:13.210
but it's hard to distract yourself

00:36:13.210 --> 00:36:14.760
from something you committed to.

00:36:14.760 --> 00:36:16.534
I knew it wasn't right for me,

00:36:16.534 --> 00:36:18.627
my heart just knew it wasn't right for me,

00:36:18.627 --> 00:36:20.760
and also I wanted to do it on good terms.

00:36:20.760 --> 00:36:22.300
I'm still a venture
partner with the firm now

00:36:22.300 --> 00:36:24.023
and it was important to me

00:36:24.023 --> 00:36:25.790
'cause I have so much respect
for the firm and the team

00:36:25.790 --> 00:36:28.640
but I also knew that for 15 years,

00:36:28.640 --> 00:36:29.473
I felt like I'd

00:36:29.473 --> 00:36:30.722
hung up my shoes--
- So real quick

00:36:30.722 --> 00:36:33.610
'cause I see Seth wants to make
a call, so what did you do?

00:36:33.610 --> 00:36:34.443
You got out and?

00:36:34.443 --> 00:36:35.890
- So I jumped out, I was like,

00:36:35.890 --> 00:36:38.333
okay, what do I want to do?
What am I most happy doing?

00:36:38.333 --> 00:36:40.210
Honestly, it's building
products for creative people.

00:36:40.210 --> 00:36:41.150
It's what I've always been doing,

00:36:41.150 --> 00:36:43.550
even though I had sold the company,

00:36:43.550 --> 00:36:45.630
why do I have to stop doing that?

00:36:45.630 --> 00:36:47.220
- So did you think about
starting a new company?

00:36:47.220 --> 00:36:48.485
What did you think about?

00:36:48.485 --> 00:36:49.547
- I thought about a lot of these things.

00:36:49.547 --> 00:36:51.830
And I realized that my perfect happy place

00:36:51.830 --> 00:36:53.610
was building products for creative people,

00:36:53.610 --> 00:36:54.870
working with people that I love

00:36:54.870 --> 00:36:56.750
and investing as something I do

00:36:56.750 --> 00:36:58.740
as sort of the side hustle, if you will.

00:36:58.740 --> 00:37:00.150
And so I said, well what's wrong

00:37:00.150 --> 00:37:01.923
with just going back and doing that?

00:37:01.923 --> 00:37:03.800
And at the time, Shantanu,
the CEO of Adobe,

00:37:03.800 --> 00:37:04.850
had come to me and was like,

00:37:04.850 --> 00:37:07.188
I'm thinking about this
cheap product officer role,

00:37:07.188 --> 00:37:09.330
would you consider coming back?

00:37:09.330 --> 00:37:10.200
And at first, I'm like,

00:37:10.200 --> 00:37:11.610
I don't know if I would ever come back

00:37:11.610 --> 00:37:12.700
to a place I already left

00:37:12.700 --> 00:37:14.700
and extracted myself
from and whatever else.

00:37:14.700 --> 00:37:15.800
And then I realized,

00:37:15.800 --> 00:37:17.070
if I feel like there's a strategy

00:37:17.070 --> 00:37:17.960
that gets me excited

00:37:17.960 --> 00:37:19.880
and I have the team and the founder DNA

00:37:19.880 --> 00:37:21.760
that I still have within the company

00:37:21.760 --> 00:37:23.810
because of Behance's acquisition,

00:37:23.810 --> 00:37:25.160
I said, why not, let's jump in.

00:37:25.160 --> 00:37:26.450
And so now we're kind of thinking

00:37:26.450 --> 00:37:28.320
about the future of the creative world

00:37:28.320 --> 00:37:30.150
as well as thinking about this question

00:37:30.150 --> 00:37:32.420
around digital marketing
and creative tools

00:37:32.420 --> 00:37:34.060
and how creatives can come together.

00:37:34.060 --> 00:37:35.000
Bring it back to the question.

00:37:35.000 --> 00:37:37.300
- I don't know where the
energy was for his question.

00:37:37.300 --> 00:37:38.860
My answer is, you don't.

00:37:38.860 --> 00:37:41.250
So first of all, there's
industries but there's no,

00:37:41.250 --> 00:37:42.600
this is back to managing people,

00:37:42.600 --> 00:37:46.020
okay cool, apparel companies
have figured it out.

00:37:46.020 --> 00:37:48.740
Not all apparel companies.

00:37:48.740 --> 00:37:50.560
Cement companies have not figured it out.

00:37:50.560 --> 00:37:52.290
Well there's some guy in Minnesota

00:37:52.290 --> 00:37:55.120
who's killing it selling
cement on Facebook.

00:37:55.120 --> 00:37:56.900
I think the answer is you don't.

00:37:56.900 --> 00:37:58.880
This is what I love about the game.

00:37:58.880 --> 00:38:03.320
I as a pied piper

